					}
				},
				{
					"@type": "Question",
					"name": "Why should I use DuckDuckGo instead of Google?",
					"acceptedAnswer": {
						"@type": "Answer",
						"text": "Use DuckDuckGo if you want privacy. Google tracks every search you make and builds detailed profiles. DuckDuckGo doesn't track you, doesn't create profiles, and shows the same results to everyone. It also has fewer AI features forced on you."
					}
				},
				{
					"@type": "Question",
					"name": "Is DuckDuckGo search results as good as Google?",
					"acceptedAnswer": {
						"@type": "Answer",
						"text": "For most searches, DuckDuckGo results are good enough. Google excels at complex, specific, or local queries. DuckDuckGo uses Bing's index plus its own crawler. The quality gap has narrowed significantly in recent years."
					}
				},
				{
					"@type": "Question",
					"name": "Does DuckDuckGo have AI features like Google?",
					"acceptedAnswer": {
						"@type": "Answer",
						"text": "DuckDuckGo has optional AI features (Duck.ai, AI Assist) but they can be disabled. Use noai.duckduckgo.com for a completely AI-free experience. Google's AI Overview is on by default and harder to turn off."
					}
				}
			]
		}
		</script>
	</head>
	<body>
		<div class="container">
			<nav class="breadcrumb">
				<a href="/">Home</a>
				<span>/</span>
				<a href="/guides">Guides</a>
				<span>/</span>
				<span>DuckDuckGo vs Google</span>
			</nav>

			<h1>DuckDuckGo vs Google</h1>
			<p class="intro">A detailed comparison of privacy, search quality, features, and AI integration.</p>

			<nav class="toc">
				<h2>Contents</h2>
				<ol>
					<li><a href="#overview">Quick Overview</a></li>
					<li><a href="#privacy">Privacy Comparison</a></li>
					<li><a href="#search-quality">Search Quality</a></li>
					<li><a href="#ai-features">AI Features</a></li>
					<li><a href="#features">Unique Features</a></li>
					<li><a href="#which-to-use">Which Should You Use?</a></li>
					<li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
				</ol>
			</nav>

			<section id="overview">
				<h2>Quick Overview</h2>
				<div class="table-wrapper">
					<table>
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th>Feature</th>
								<th>DuckDuckGo</th>
								<th>Google</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>Privacy</strong></td>
								<td class="status-good">Excellent - No tracking</td>
								<td class="status-bad">Poor - Extensive tracking</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>Search Quality</strong></td>
								<td>Good</td>
								<td class="status-good">Excellent</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>AI Features</strong></td>
								<td class="status-good">Optional, can disable</td>
								<td class="status-bad">On by default</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>Index Source</strong></td>
								<td>Bing + own crawler</td>
								<td>Own (largest)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>Personalization</strong></td>
								<td>None (by design)</td>
								<td>Heavy (tracks you)</td>
							</tr>
							<tr>
								<td><strong>Ads</strong></td>
								<td>Non-tracking ads</td>
								<td>Targeted ads</td>
							</tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>

				<div class="verdict-box">
					<h3>The Verdict</h3>
					<p><strong>Choose DuckDuckGo</strong> if privacy matters to you and you want to avoid tracking. For everyday searches, it works well.</p>
					<p><strong>Choose Google</strong> if you need the absolute best results for complex queries and don't mind being tracked. Consider using <a href="/guides/disable-ai-overview-google">udm=14 to disable AI</a>.</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="privacy">
				<h2>Privacy Comparison</h2>

				<div class="comparison-grid">
					<div class="comparison-card ddg">
						<h3>DuckDuckGo</h3>
						<span class="comparison-tag green">Privacy Winner</span>
						<ul>
							<li>No search history stored</li>
							<li>No IP address logging</li>
							<li>No user profiles created</li>
							<li>No cross-site tracking</li>
							<li>Same results for everyone</li>
							<li>Non-tracking ads only</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="comparison-card google">
						<h3>Google</h3>
						<span class="comparison-tag red">Privacy Concerns</span>
						<ul>
							<li>Stores all search history</li>
							<li>Logs IP addresses</li>
							<li>Builds detailed profiles</li>
							<li>Tracks across all Google services</li>
							<li>Personalized results (filter bubble)</li>
							<li>Targeted advertising</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3>What Google Knows About You</h3>
				<p>Every Google search is linked to your profile. Google knows:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Your health concerns (medical searches)</li>
					<li>Your financial situation (loan, debt searches)</li>
					<li>Your political views (news and candidate searches)</li>
					<li>Your relationships (people you search for)</li>
					<li>Your location history</li>
					<li>Products you're interested in buying</li>
				</ul>

				<h3>DuckDuckGo's Approach</h3>
				<p>DuckDuckGo simply doesn't collect this data. They make money from contextual ads based on your current search, not your profile. Search for "running shoes" and you might see a shoe ad - but that ad doesn't follow you around the web.</p>
			</section>

			<section id="search-quality">
				<h2>Search Quality</h2>

				<h3>Where Google Wins</h3>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Complex queries</strong> - Technical, specific, or nuanced searches</li>
					<li><strong>Local results</strong> - "restaurants near me" type searches</li>
					<li><strong>Recent content</strong> - Breaking news and current events</li>
					<li><strong>Image search</strong> - Larger index, better reverse image search</li>
					<li><strong>Spell correction</strong> - Better at understanding typos</li>
				</ul>

				<h3>Where DuckDuckGo Performs Well</h3>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>General searches</strong> - Everyday queries work fine</li>
					<li><strong>Shopping</strong> - Good product results without tracking</li>
					<li><strong>Programming</strong> - Developer searches work well</li>
					<li><strong>Wikipedia/reference</strong> - Quick answers from reliable sources</li>
					<li><strong>Privacy-sensitive searches</strong> - No filter bubble distortion</li>
				</ul>

				<h3>The Reality</h3>
				<p>For 90% of everyday searches, you won't notice a difference. The gap appears with:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Very specific technical queries</li>
					<li>Local business searches</li>
					<li>Searches requiring recent information</li>
				</ul>

				<div class="tip">
					<strong>Tip:</strong> Use DuckDuckGo's <code>!g</code> bang to quickly search Google for the rare query that needs it: <code>my query !g</code>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="ai-features">
				<h2>AI Features Comparison</h2>

				<div class="comparison-grid">
					<div class="comparison-card ddg">
						<h3>DuckDuckGo AI</h3>
						<span class="comparison-tag green">Optional</span>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Duck.ai</strong> - AI chat (separate feature)</li>
							<li><strong>Search Assist</strong> - Optional AI answers</li>
							<li><strong>AI Images</strong> - Can be filtered out</li>
							<li>Not forced on users</li>
							<li>Official AI-free subdomain available</li>
						</ul>
						<p class="disable-info">Use: <code>noai.duckduckgo.com</code> or params <code>kbg=-1&kbe=-1</code></p>
					</div>
					<div class="comparison-card google">
						<h3>Google AI</h3>
						<span class="comparison-tag red">Default On</span>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>AI Overview</strong> - Summaries at top</li>
							<li>On by default for most queries</li>
							<li>No official toggle to disable</li>
							<li>Can be slow to load</li>
							<li>Sometimes contains errors</li>
						</ul>
						<p class="disable-info">Disable with: <code>udm=14</code> or <a href="/">Unslop Search</a></p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3>If You Want No AI</h3>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>DuckDuckGo HTML:</strong> <code>duckduckgo.com/html</code> - No AI, no JavaScript</li>
					<li><strong>DuckDuckGo Lite:</strong> <code>lite.duckduckgo.com</code> - Minimal version</li>
					<li><strong>Google with udm=14:</strong> Forces "Web" results only</li>
					<li><strong>Unslop Search:</strong> <a href="/">Applies AI-disabling parameters automatically</a></li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section id="features">
				<h2>Unique Features</h2>

				<h3>DuckDuckGo Exclusive Features</h3>

				<div class="feature-highlight">
					<h4>!Bangs</h4>
					<p>Search other sites directly from DuckDuckGo:</p>
					<ul>
						<li><code>!w cats</code> - Search Wikipedia for cats</li>
						<li><code>!yt music</code> - Search YouTube for music</li>
						<li><code>!a laptop</code> - Search Amazon for laptop</li>
						<li><code>!g query</code> - Search Google (when you need it)</li>
					</ul>
					<p>There are thousands of bangs for nearly every website.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="feature-highlight">
					<h4>Email Protection</h4>
					<p>Generate @duck.com email addresses that forward to your real email, stripping trackers. Free and built into DuckDuckGo.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="feature-highlight">
					<h4>App Tracking Protection</h4>
					<p>DuckDuckGo's mobile app blocks trackers in other apps, not just in search.</p>
				</div>

				<h3>Google Exclusive Features</h3>

				<div class="feature-highlight">
					<h4>Knowledge Graph</h4>
					<p>Rich information panels for people, places, and things. More comprehensive than DuckDuckGo's instant answers.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="feature-highlight">
					<h4>Integration</h4>
					<p>Deep integration with Gmail, Google Maps, Google Calendar, etc. Convenient if you use Google services (but increases tracking).</p>
				</div>

				<div class="feature-highlight">
					<h4>Local Search</h4>
					<p>Superior local business results with reviews, hours, and real-time information.</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="which-to-use">
				<h2>Which Should You Use?</h2>

				<div class="recommendation-grid">
					<div class="recommendation-card">
						<h3>Use DuckDuckGo If...</h3>
						<ul>
							<li>Privacy is important to you</li>
							<li>You're tired of targeted ads</li>
							<li>You want the same results as everyone else</li>
							<li>You want to avoid AI summaries</li>
							<li>You like !bangs for quick site searches</li>
							<li>Your searches are mostly general</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="recommendation-card">
						<h3>Use Google (with udm=14) If...</h3>
						<ul>
							<li>You need the best possible results</li>
							<li>You do lots of local searches</li>
							<li>You search for very specific technical topics</li>
							<li>You're okay with tracking (or use incognito)</li>
							<li>You heavily use Google's other services</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="method-box recommended">
					<span class="method-tag">Best of Both</span>
					<h3>Use Unslop Search</h3>
					<p>Get the flexibility to switch between search engines with AI disabled on all of them:</p>
					<ul>
						<li>Use DuckDuckGo as your default</li>
						<li>Switch to Google when needed (AI auto-disabled)</li>
						<li>Try Brave, Startpage, or others</li>
						<li>All with AI features turned off</li>
					</ul>
					<a href="/" class="cta-button">Try Unslop Search</a>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section id="faq">
				<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

				<div class="faq-item">
					<h3>Is DuckDuckGo better than Google?</h3>
					<p>For privacy, yes - DuckDuckGo doesn't track you at all. For search quality, Google still leads on complex queries. For everyday searches, DuckDuckGo is good enough for most people while protecting your privacy.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="faq-item">
					<h3>Why should I use DuckDuckGo instead of Google?</h3>
					<p>The main reason is privacy. Google builds detailed profiles from your searches. DuckDuckGo doesn't track you, doesn't create profiles, and doesn't show personalized results. It also makes it easier to avoid AI features.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="faq-item">
					<h3>Are DuckDuckGo search results as good as Google?</h3>
					<p>For most searches, yes. DuckDuckGo uses Bing's index plus its own crawler, which works well for everyday queries. Google is better for very specific, technical, or local searches. The gap has narrowed significantly over the years.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="faq-item">
					<h3>Does DuckDuckGo have AI features like Google?</h3>
					<p>DuckDuckGo has optional AI features (Duck.ai, Search Assist) that can be disabled. The easiest way is to use <code>noai.duckduckgo.com</code> - DuckDuckGo's official AI-free subdomain. Google's AI Overview is on by default. You can also use <a href="/">Unslop Search</a> which disables AI automatically on both.</p>
				</div>

				<div class="faq-item">
					<h3>Can I use both DuckDuckGo and Google?</h3>
					<p>Yes! Use DuckDuckGo as your default for privacy, and use the <code>!g</code> bang when you need Google. Or use <a href="/">Unslop Search</a> to switch easily between engines with AI disabled on all of them.</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<div class="next-steps">
				<h2>Next Steps</h2>
				<div class="next-links">
					<a href="/guides/set-default-search-engine">
						<span>Setup</span>
						<strong>Set Your Default Search</strong>
					</a>
					<a href="/guides/disable-ai-overview-google">
						<span>Guide</span>
						<strong>Disable Google AI</strong>
					</a>
					<a href="/guides/best-private-search-engines">
						<span>Compare</span>
						<strong>More Private Engines</strong>
					</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</body>
</html>

